---
/**
 * How the sources work.
 *
 * The benchmark has a /docs section with "How we source data" and links it from
 * its footer under the heading Data sources. This is that page, built from numbers
 * read out of the articles at build time rather than asserted.
 *
 * It deliberately includes the part a sourcing page usually omits: the sources we
 * tried to read and could not. An advisory behind a bot wall, a PDF that would not
 * parse, a study that returned an empty body, a page that returned 403 — each is
 * recorded on the article that relied on it, and counted here. A page that only
 * lists what it read is a marketing page.
 */
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { SITE } from "../consts";
import { fitTitle, fitDescription } from "../lib/meta";

const posts = await getCollection("blog", ({ data }) => !data.draft);

const rows = posts
  .map((p) => ({
    slug: p.id,
    title: p.data.title,
    sources: p.data.sources?.length ?? 0,
    figures: p.data.figures?.length ?? 0,
    gain: p.data.information_gain,
    stakes: p.data.stakes,
    revisions: p.data.revisions?.length ?? 0,
  }))
  .sort((a, b) => b.sources - a.sources);

const totalSources = rows.reduce((a, r) => a + r.sources, 0);
const totalFigures = rows.reduce((a, r) => a + r.figures, 0);
const minSources = Math.min(...rows.map((r) => r.sources));
const maxSources = Math.max(...rows.map((r) => r.sources));
const priced = rows.filter((r) => r.stakes === "priced").length;
const calculated = rows.filter((r) => r.gain === "custom-calculation").length;

const title = "How the sources work";
const description =
  "Every figure on this site carries a value, unit, period, named source and retrieval date. Here is how many, from which registers, and which sources we could not read.";
---

<BaseLayout title={`${fitTitle(title, "", SITE.name)} — ${SITE.name}`} description={description}>
  <div class="container">
    <main id="main">
      <article>
        <header class="article-header">
          <h1>{title}</h1>
          <p class="standfirst">
            Every figure here carries a value, a unit, a period, a named source and
            the date it was retrieved. This page counts them, names the registers
            behind them, and lists what we tried to read and could not.
          </p>
        </header>

        <div class="prose">
          <div class="tracker-summary">
            <div class="tracker-stat">
              <span class="tracker-num">{totalFigures}</span>
              <span class="tracker-lbl">figures published</span>
            </div>
            <div class="tracker-stat">
              <span class="tracker-num">{totalSources}</span>
              <span class="tracker-lbl">named sources</span>
            </div>
            <div class="tracker-stat">
              <span class="tracker-num">{minSources}–{maxSources}</span>
              <span class="tracker-lbl">sources per article</span>
            </div>
            <div class="tracker-stat">
              <span class="tracker-num">{calculated}</span>
              <span class="tracker-lbl">carry a cost calculation</span>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h2>The record every figure carries</h2>
          <p>
            A number without a date is a number you cannot check. So every figure in
            every article is recorded with five things, and the article states all
            five:
          </p>
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Field</th><th>What it means</th><th>Example</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td>Value</td>
                <td>The number itself</td>
                <td><code>EUR 75.36</code></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Unit</td>
                <td>What it is per, so it cannot be misread as a total</td>
                <td>per tonne of CO<sub>2</sub>e</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Period</td>
                <td>The window the figure applies to</td>
                <td>Q1 2026, published 7 April 2026</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Source</td>
                <td>A named body, not a trade-press summary</td>
                <td>European Commission, DG TAXUD</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Retrieved</td>
                <td>The date we read it, so staleness is visible</td>
                <td>2026-10-03</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <p>
            All {totalFigures} published figures carry a retrieval date. Where a
            rate is published by a government and we could not reach the primary
            text, the article names the accessible source it did use and says so.
          </p>

          <h2>Which registers the numbers come from</h2>
          <p>
            Legal text, gazettes and official statistics, not summaries of them. A
            law-firm note or a wire report is used as a lead to find the instrument, and
            when it is the only thing available the article says that too.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Bank Negara Malaysia</strong> — policy rate, exchange rate, reserves and financial stability.</li>
            <li><strong>Department of Statistics Malaysia</strong> — prices, trade, output, employment and household income.</li>
            <li><strong>Ministry of Finance</strong> — budget documents, tax and subsidy measures.</li>
            <li><strong>MITI and MIDA</strong> — trade measures, agreements, investment approvals.</li>
            <li><strong>Royal Malaysian Customs (JKDM)</strong> — duties and sales-and-service tax, when the document can be read.</li>
            <li><strong>P.U. (A) and P.U. (B) gazettes, and <em>Akta</em> numbers</strong> — the instrument, its schedule and its commencement date.</li>
            <li><strong>IMF, World Bank, WTO, OECD, BIS</strong> — the international series and the country reviews.</li>
            <li><strong>US Federal Reserve, USTR, the Federal Register, the European Commission and ECB</strong> — the world-side decisions that move the ringgit and Malaysian trade.</li>
            <li><strong>ISEAS – Yusof Ishak Institute and ISIS Malaysia</strong> — regional and strategic analysis, attributed as analysis and never used as a substitute for a figure.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The reachability of every one of these hosts is re-measured on a schedule and the result is
            committed to the repository, failures included. Three of them answer but cannot be quoted —
            an agency that serves an English page and a Malay PDF is reachable and still unusable, and
            saying so is part of the record.
          </p>

          <h2>What we tried to read and could not</h2>
          <p>
            This is the part a sourcing page usually leaves out. While checking
            competitors for each article, sources are recorded even when they cannot
            be opened, and the article notes the gap instead of quietly working
            around it. Across the article set so far:
          </p>
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Outcome</th><th>What it looked like</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td>Paywalled or bot-blocked</td>
                <td>A law firm advisory returned HTTP 403 behind a challenge, and a mirrored copy did the same. The advisory's argument reached us through a trade-press quotation, which is attributed as such.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>PDF that would not parse</td>
                <td>A tax advisory published only as a PDF. Recorded on the article as attempted and not read, with a note that it is probably the strongest analysis in that search.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Empty response</td>
                <td>A parliamentary research study returned HTTP 202 with no body.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Definition page absent</td>
                <td>An index publisher's definition page returned only its navigation shell, and the English equivalent returned 404. The question it would have answered is stated as open on the article rather than inferred from a sibling document.</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <p>
            <strong>A missing or uncertain figure is safer than a confident guess.</strong>
            Where a gap could not be closed, the article says which question is
            unanswered and what would settle it, and the reader is told to get it in
            writing rather than take our reading.
          </p>
          <p>
            The same record is published as data at{" "}
            <code>/unreadable.json</code> — one entry per
            host, with the status returned, the date it was tested, what it was wanted
            for, and what was used instead. It is there so the claim that every figure
            is traceable can be checked in the direction that is usually left out. The
            file distinguishes a 412 gateway from a 404, because one means the host
            refuses automated reads and the other means the path is gone, and the two
            call for different responses.
          </p>
          <p>
            It is deliberately not in the sitemap. A sitemap is a list of pages for a
            search engine, and a JSON payload in one is noise — the link above is how
            this file is meant to be found. Where a figure in an article rests on a
            source listed here, the article says so and names what was used instead.
          </p>

          <h2>Per-article record</h2>
          <p>
            Counts read straight from the published files, so this table cannot drift
            from what the articles contain.
          </p>
          <table class="tracker-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Article</th>
                <th>Sources</th>
                <th>Figures</th>
                <th>Gain</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {rows.map((r) => (
                <tr>
                  <td><a href={`/blog/${r.slug}/`}>{r.title}</a></td>
                  <td class="tracker-num-cell">{r.sources}</td>
                  <td class="tracker-num-cell">{r.figures}</td>
                  <td class="tracker-meta-cell">{r.gain.replace(/-/g, " ")}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>

          <h2>Where a figure is our own arithmetic</h2>
          <p>
            {calculated} of {rows.length} articles declare their information gain as a
            custom calculation, which means the arithmetic — duty per unit, cost per
            container, cost per tonne — is ours rather than quoted. Those articles
            show the working, name the inputs as assumptions, and use the same models
            so a figure here can
            be recomputed rather than taken on trust. {priced} of {rows.length} are
            marked as priced, meaning the cost lands on a buyer's invoice rather than
            merely indicating a direction.
          </p>

          <h2>When a figure changes</h2>
          <p>
            Each article carries a date on which it will be re-read against the
            primary source. When a figure is corrected, the correction is listed on
            the article itself with its own date rather than being edited in silence.
            Every article carries its own re-check date.
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>
    </main>

    <aside class="sidebar">
      <div class="widget">
        <h2>Check it yourself</h2>
        <p>
          Every article lists its own sources with dates. The registers are linked
          there, not summarised here.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="widget">
        <h2>Work out your own cost</h2>
        <p>
          The landed-cost calculator uses the same models
          that produce the figures in these articles.
        </p>
      </div>
    </aside>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
